<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samurai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The samurai were the hereditary military class of pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> they became a largely administrative and bureaucratic elite in a long period of peace, paid fixed stipends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and expected to follow codes of loyalty and service. Commercial growth eroded their economic position, and the class was abolished after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samurai.jpg" alt="Samurai" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samurai_on_horseback0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the late 8th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had a national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army, which the imperial court dismantled, ending it by 792. Conscripts were seen as unreliable, and in the war against the Emishi, who relied on mounted warriors, professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> from wealthy families decided most battles. The government recruited men who already had those skills, acquired through private training funded by their families. The word samurai is thought to come from saburau, to serve, and so means a retainer or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>; the proper term for a professional warrior is bushi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> were bushi but were not called samurai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Minamoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clans</a>, both with court positions, became rivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> became the first bushi given a senior court rank, as chief minister in 1167. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180 to 1185 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> ruled through a military government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, and in 1192 he created the gokenin class of warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, who owed the shogun military service in exchange for privileges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a>, which began in 1467, brought down the Ashikaga shogunate and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> fought each other.</p><p>After the Battle of Sekigahara Tokugawa Ieyasu was declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1603, and after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615 there was a period of peace for 250 years. Under the Tokugawa the samurai first became a truly hereditary class, a process begun by Hideyoshi with the Sword Hunt of 1588 and the Separation Edict of 1591. With no warfare they gradually lost their military function; many served as officials, police, teachers or domain administrators, while keeping stipends and the right to bear arms.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the abolition of the domains, conscription, the ending of hereditary stipends and the removal of privileges such as wearing swords dismantled the basis of samurai status. Former feudal lords became kazoku, nobility, and most former warriors became shizoku, classes that remained legally recognised until 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The samurai class was highly stratified, by the rank of one's lord and the size of one's stipend. Below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatamoto">hatamoto</a>, who had the right of audience with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, and then the gokenin. Samurai trained from a young age in swordsmanship, archery and horsemanship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and, to a lesser extent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> influenced their culture, and the works <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagakure">Hagakure</a> and Gorin no Sho, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, contributed to the development of bushidō.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samurai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Another typhoon destroyed at least one-third of the Mongol force, and troops left ashore were slaughtered by the <b>samurai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1609 the Shimazu clan, with the consent of Tokugawa Ieyasu, sent about 3,000 <b>samurai</b> armed with arquebuses in more than 100 ships under Kabayama Hisataka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The merchant class (chonin) was placed at the bottom of the Confucian order beneath <b>samurai</b>, farmers and artisans, yet it accumulated wealth that increasingly outstripped that of the samurai, whose fixed rice stipends were eroded by commercial change.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Japan Confucian ideas arrived with Chinese writing and learning by the 6th century; under the Tokugawa shogunate the Neo-Confucianism of Zhu Xi became the official teaching of the shogunate’s academy and was taught in schools for the <b>samurai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Edo period Japan was governed by a rigid order of inherited rank: the Emperor and his Court at the top, then the shōgun with the rōjū and the daimyō, then the <b>samurai</b>, farmers, artisans and merchants.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In Heian Japan the Fujiwara family dominated the court by marrying its daughters to emperors, and in 1016 Fujiwara no Michinaga became regent for his grandson Emperor Go-Ichijō; later the daimyō, warrior lords with their own <b>samurai</b>, held real power.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
